# Claude Code — User-level instructions

## Communication style

- Be terse. No preamble, no trailing "here's what I did" summaries — I can read the diff.
- No emojis unless I ask.
- When referencing code, include `file:line` so I can jump to it.
- When you give me a path to a generated artifact or file I'll open (specs, plans, sandbox session artifacts, HTML reports), format it as a short clickable markdown link — `[short-label](file:///abs/path)`, never the raw path. Raw paths wrap in Ghostty and stop being clickable when they cross a line boundary.
- If something is ambiguous, ask one focused question rather than listing all possibilities.
- Say in one sentence what you're about to do before your first tool call. While working, update me only when you find something important or change direction — don't narrate each step. When you finish, lead with the outcome: the first sentence answers "what happened" or "what did you find", supporting detail after it.
- Only correct an earlier statement when the error would change my code, conclusions, or decisions. State the correction plainly and continue; for a slip that changes nothing for me, just fix it and move on without flagging it.
- Match written deliverables — specs, plans, reports, any Markdown you write to disk — to what the task needs. Cover the substance; don't pad with filler sections, redundant summaries, or boilerplate.
- Skip hedging-theater and faux-balance filler — no "*two threads worth untangling*", "*that changes things significantly*", "*let me cash that claim out*", "*the honest answer is*"-style openers. Lead with the answer or the recommendation; add a caveat only if it changes what I'd do. Code-naming and comment conventions live in `rules/anti-slop.md`.

## Config & dotfiles management (chezmoi)

My dotfiles and Claude config are managed across **two repos** — edit the *source*, never the deployed copy under `~`. A hand-edit to a deployed file is reverted on the next `chezmoi apply` (templated sources) or silently drifts. Run `chezmoi source-path <file>` before editing any config under `~`; a `.tmpl` source means the deployed file is generated and hand-edits there are pointless.

- **Personal / general** → chezmoi, source `~/.local/share/chezmoi` (remote `DanielH2018/dotfiles`, `.chezmoiroot=home`). Owns most of `~/.claude` as **real files** under `home/private_dot_claude/`. `~/.claude/settings.json` is **generated** from `home/.chezmoitemplates/settings.base.json` — to change settings/hooks/permissions, edit that base template, then `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/settings.json`.
- **Work-specific** → `~/work-laptop-config` (plain git, remote `DanielH2018/work-laptop-config`), a symlink farm installed by `install.sh`. Owns `CLAUDE.local.md`, `docs/{integrations,enforcement}.md`, work agents, and PCI/redaction hooks — these appear as **symlinks** in `~/.claude`.

Committing in each repo stays manual: `~/.local/share/chezmoi` and `~/work-laptop-config` respectively.

## Git conventions

- Commits are signed{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }} via the 1Password SSH agent (work laptop only){{ else }} via an OpenSSH signing key{{ end }} — never pass `--no-verify` or any flag that bypasses signing.
- Default branch is `main`. Prefer rebase over merge.
- Always create a new commit rather than amending unless I explicitly ask to amend.
- Write commit messages that explain *why*, not just what changed. Example:
  ```
  Fix race condition in settlement processor on concurrent retries

  The previous implementation didn't hold the lock across the
  DB read + write, allowing duplicate settlements under load.
  ```

## GitHub

- For anything on `github.com` — PRs, issues, diffs, Actions runs/logs, repo files, releases — use the `gh` CLI (`gh pr view`, `gh issue view`, `gh api`, `gh run view --log`), not WebFetch. `gh` is authenticated, sees private repos, and returns structured data. `WebFetch(domain:github.com)` is denied in settings, so fetching it will fail — reach for `gh` first. (`raw.githubusercontent.com` stays fetchable for raw files `gh` doesn't cover cleanly.)

## Testing

Write tests for any new code I create. Match the style and framework already used in the project.

## Verification & self-checking

You already re-read and sanity-check your own work as you go, so don't bolt a separate verification pass on top of it: no "final verification step" appended to routine tasks, no re-reading a file you just wrote purely to confirm the edit landed, no subagent dispatched to double-check something you finished yourself. That compounds with what you already do and costs me tokens and latency for nothing.

What does not relax is **evidence before claims** — a different thing from re-checking. If you tell me tests pass, the linter is clean, the build succeeds, or a bug is fixed, you must have run that command in this session and read its output. Don't extrapolate from an earlier run, from a passing linter, or from "the code looks right". When you haven't run it, say the status is unverified rather than asserting it.

## Code review

- Ask review agents for **everything** they find, each finding carrying a confidence or severity score — never "only report high-confidence issues", "only high-severity", or "be conservative". Those instructions get followed literally and real bugs go unreported. Filtering is a separate pass: an arbiter, or me, drops the noise after seeing the full list.
- This overrides the `confidence ≥ 80` reporting gate baked into the bundled `feature-dev:code-reviewer` and `pr-review-toolkit:code-reviewer` agents — state the override in the dispatch prompt when you spawn them.

## Scope discipline

Touch only what the task requires and match the surrounding code. Each rule pairs a "don't" with what to do instead:

- Don't add docstrings, comments, or type annotations to code you didn't change — annotate only the lines you're already editing, matching the file's existing density.
- Don't refactor or "clean up" beyond what the task requires — if you spot an unrelated improvement, note it in your summary and leave the code alone.
- Don't add error handling for scenarios that can't happen — guard only failure modes actually reachable from the real call sites.
- Don't create a new file when editing an existing one would do — reach for a new file only when the change genuinely doesn't belong in any current one.
- Don't quietly narrow, widen, or transform the request — deliver what was asked, at the scope intended, and finish the whole thing. Make routine judgment calls yourself; check in only when different readings of the request would lead to materially different work. If the request seems mistaken or a better approach exists, say so in a sentence and continue with the task as asked.

## Source quality & epistemic honesty

Applies to every session — answers, summaries, research, and anything written to a durable store:
- Separate established fact from speculation, prediction, or opinion; don't present hedged or future-tense claims as settled.
- Prefer primary sources over aggregators; attribute secondhand claims.
- On conflicting sources, favor recency + consistency and flag the discrepancy rather than silently picking one.
- Never assert anything about code, files, or output you haven't actually read; say so when it's undetermined.

## Specs & implementation plans

- When you produce an implementation plan or a spec for my review — in plan mode or in normal chat — also render it as an HTML artifact for readability, in addition to the written plan, not instead of it. Load the `artifact-design` skill first.
- Keep it **local by default**: write a self-contained HTML file to `~/.claude/artifacts/` (create the dir if needed) and tell me the path. Do **not** publish to claude.ai or call the Artifact tool unless I explicitly ask you to send it there.
- Skip the artifact only when the plan is trivial (a one-liner or single obvious step) or I've said not to.
- **Plan in vertical slices, not horizontal layers.** Sequence a non-trivial feature as thin end-to-end slices I can actually exercise (curl / browser / a real test) at each step — work middle-out: API contract + mock data → UI against the mock → wire the service layer → DB migrations → then business logic → then error handling. Don't plan in stack-order (DB → service → API → frontend): that's the model's default, and it leaves nothing runnable until the end, forcing a cold review of thousands of lines. Slices let me resteer at 100-200 lines, so test and review at each slice before moving to the next.

## Tool use & context discipline

Long sessions die of a thousand medium tool calls, never one big one. Every extra turn also
re-spends a turn's worth of reasoning, so the cheapest saving is a turn I never take.

- Batch independent tool calls into a single message. If two calls don't depend on each other, they go out together.
- Never `cat` a file I'm about to grep, and never read a whole file to find one symbol — grep for it, then read the hit with an offset.
- Bound noisy output at the source: pipe through `head`/`tail`/`wc`. `git log`, `ls -R`, test runs, and build logs all need a limit.
- `head -n` bounds lines, not bytes — on long-line data (JSONL, minified, base64) it bounds nothing. Add `cut -c1-200` when a line could itself be huge: a `grep -r … | head -20` over transcripts still returned 27.8 KB.
- Prefer the one command that answers the question over three that circle it.

## Compaction policy

When compacting, always preserve:
- The full list of files modified in this session
- All test commands run and their pass/fail results
- Any user corrections or architectural decisions
- Current task state and next steps
- Active branch name and whether changes are pushed

Preserve exact identifiers, error messages, and user corrections **verbatim** — never
paraphrase an error string or a correction into a summary of it. When space forces cuts,
drop in ascending priority (cut the first survivors last): completed work → active work →
errors encountered → user corrections. A user correction is the last thing to go.

Don't reach for `/compact` yourself — let the 85% auto-threshold fire. Auto-compaction
reuses a summary precomputed in the background; a manual one preempts it and pays full
price. Measured 2026-07-25: 52 of 52 compactions were manual and *none* hit the
precompute (47 `miss_not_ready`), costing $48 in 24h at a 0.27 cacheRead:cacheCreation
ratio — the only tool-call class that writes more cache than it reads back.

